openai-api conformance fixtures
===============================

Witnesses for `tests/adapter_conformance.py`. Format: `rc=<int>`, optional
`category=` (required on every `*unavailable*` file), then `--- stdout ---`
and `--- stderr ---`.

What the chain spawns is `python -I -c ... skodun.adapters.openai_api_runner`,
so these describe the RUNNER's wire format, not the raw Chat Completions
envelope: a single contract-shaped JSON object on stdout, and on stderr the
machine `SKODUN_API_USAGE ` line plus any short reason.

That distinction is what this directory got wrong before issue #99. Both
degraded fixtures asserted wording nothing in this adapter's path can emit —
`truncated response from upstream`, and `envelope refused`, which belongs to
`junie_runner` and to an envelope-staging wrapper `openai-api` does not have.
`test_degraded_fixtures_are_recognised` passed against strings only the
fixture author had written, which is the circular green tick the
`unavailable_quota` rule below warns about, on the degradation axis.

Provenance
----------

healthy.txt / healthy_noisy_stderr.txt / refuter_healthy.txt
    SYNTHESIZED in the runner's accepted shape. No live capture.

degraded_stderr.txt / degraded_truncated_answer.txt
    CAPTURED from `openai_api_runner.main` itself, run against a mocked HTTP
    response carrying `finish_reason: "length"` and a payload cut mid-string;
    the token counts and the request id are then replaced with plausible ones.
    So the `rc`, the line ORDER and the wording are the runner's own, not an
    author's recollection of them -- which is what the first draft of these
    two got wrong in a small way that matters here more than most: it declared
    `rc=2` where `main` really exits `1`, in a directory whose entire job is
    fidelity to the runner's wire behaviour.

    The pair is the two shapes that matter, and they are not the same case.
    `_truncated_answer` is the one worth having: a payload that VALIDATES
    beside a runner line saying the model stopped early, which is the only way
    this adapter can return a review that parses and must not be trusted.
    `_stderr` is the commoner one, where truncation landed mid-JSON and the
    extraction failed too.

unavailable_auth.txt / unavailable_model.txt / unavailable_quota.txt
    SYNTHESIZED from the runner's own `auth failure:` / `model failure:` /
    `rate limit:` prefixes, which it writes verbatim (see `main`). The wording
    AFTER those prefixes is the API's, quoted from its documented error
    strings, not invented to match the table.

    Standing obligation, as for every adapter: when a live failure of each
    kind is captured, replace these with the sanitized real stderr.
